# Test: how do people choose?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "A test across the whole block, with new cases"
next: {"title":"Why do firms exist at all if markets work so well?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/106-why-do-firms-exist-at-all-if-markets.cast"}

## Ella's month
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Ella should hire you to plan her month.
    good: A sharp eye for where curves touch.
    poor: Worth another look at the two pulls.
    none: Even the ferry company would want a recount.
use: indifference_budget
with:
    labels: true
    label_size: 22
    axis_numbers: true
    income: 120
    price_x: 15
    price_x_before: 10
    price_y: 6
    preference: balanced
    curves: 1
    x_good: Ferry trips
    y_good: Films
    x_max: 14
    y_max: 26
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 115
      w: 700
      h: 560

    dot pX #3d3833 at.data [2,16.67] hidden true
    dot pY #3d3833 at.data [6,10] hidden true
    dot pZ #3d3833 at.data [10,3.33] hidden true
    text lX "X" at.data [2.7,18] font_size 28 hidden true
    text lY "Y" at.data [6.8,11.4] font_size 28 hidden true
    text lZ "Z" at.data [10.7,4.8] font_size 28 hidden true
    icon ella size 90 x 860 y 330 icon_look picture of ferry hidden true
    scratch prices work ["I = $120","ferry $10","cinema $6"] x 850 y 520 font_size 26 hidden true
    text fare "ferry $15" #b5482e x 850 y 560 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch save work ["now costs 1 + r","r ↑: later cheaper","but saver richer","saver: pulls fight","borrower: same way"] x 860 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Test: how do people choose?"

    hide guide_y

How do people choose? Time to test the whole block on cases we haven't met, starting with Ella, (@draw ella@) an apprentice at the boatyard.
    draw axes

B: Illustrative numbers: a hundred and twen (@draw budget_old prices_box prices_line_1 prices_line_2 prices_line_3@)ty dollars a month for ferry trips to the mainland at ten dollars, and cinema tickets at six.

Her tastes ar (@draw ic_old@)e curves like this one: every point on it is just as good to her. (@draw pX lX@) Three months (@draw pY lY@) on her budge (@draw pZ lZ@)t line. Which is her best?

    ask "Which month is Ella's best? Tap X, Y or Z." choose ["pX","pY","pZ"] answer pY
        right: Y: the only month where her curve touches the line.
        wrong: Look for the month where her curve touches the line without crossing it: here, Y.

Y, where her curve just touches the line: six trips, ten films. X and Z lie below that curve, so a swap along the line climbs higher.
    highlight pY effect circle

    fade pX lX pZ lZ to 0.2

    erase ella prices pX lX pZ lZ

    hide pY lY

B: Then the ferry  (@draw fare@)company  (@draw budget_line label_budget_old@)raises the fare to fift (@draw point_a label_point_a guide_a@)een dollars. The line swings in; her old best (@draw ic_1 optimum optimum_label guide_x@), Y, is now marked A, and her best month drops to four trips.

    quiz "The ferry fare rises from $10 to $15. Which part of Ella's cut is the substitution effect?"
        right: The cut she'd make even if kept as happy: trips now cost more films each, so she swaps toward films.
        wrong: Imagine giving her back just enough money to stay on her old curve; what she still cuts is the swap: the first answer.
        + The cut she'd make even if handed enough money to stay as happy
        * The cut that comes from being poorer
        * None: she simply can't afford the trips

Two pulls, both the (@draw budget_comp point_c label_point_c guide_c@) same way. Trips now cost more films ea (@draw substitution_effect label_substitution@)ch, so she swaps; and she's poorer, so (@draw income_effect label_income@) she trims. About one trip each.

B: Pause and drag the ferry end of the line: the dearer the fare, the further the best point slides.
    explore

    erase fare budget_comp label_budget_comp guide_c

    fade axes budget_old label_budget_old ic_old label_ic_old budget_line label_budget ic_1 label_ic_1 point_a label_point_a guide_a optimum optimum_label guide_x point_c label_point_c substitution_effect label_substitution income_effect label_income to 0.12 duration 0.4

Now stretch the same picture ac (@draw save_box save_line_1@)ross time. Ella saves for a boat, and the bank raises its interest rate.

    quiz "Ella saves for a boat, and the bank's interest rate rises. What happens to her saving?" voice b
        right: It could go either way: later gets cheaper, which pulls saving up, but a saver also gets richer, which pulls it down.
        wrong: A higher rate makes later cheaper, but it also makes a saver richer: it could go either way.
        + It could go either way
        * She surely saves more
        * She surely saves less

B: A higher rate makes later (@draw save_line_2@) cheaper, so she'd save more. But it also makes a (@draw save_line_3@) saver richer, and richer people spend more today.

So for a saver the two (@draw save_line_4@) pulls fight, and only her tastes say which wins. For a borro (@draw save_line_5@)wer they point the same way.

    erase save

    fade axes budget_old label_budget_old ic_old label_ic_old budget_line label_budget ic_1 label_ic_1 point_a label_point_a guide_a optimum optimum_label guide_x point_c label_point_c substitution_effect label_substitution income_effect label_income to 1 duration 0.4

One picture, then: a line for what you can afford, curves for what you want, and two pulls whenever a price moves.
    highlight budget_line ic_1 optimum

## Risk, losses, later
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Not a single snooze in that round.
    good: Mostly sorted, with a little present bias.
    poor: The four boxes are worth another look.
    none: 'Defaults win again: nothing chosen.'

    grid grid gap 30 columns 2 fit.h 360 fit.w 600 fit.x 40 fit.y 250 hidden true
    box n1 "Risk aversion" icon "game die" in grid hidden true
    box n2 "Loss aversion" icon "balance scale" in grid hidden true
    box n3 "Present bias" icon "alarm clock" in grid hidden true
    box n4 "Defaults" icon "check box with check" in grid hidden true
    scratch wage work ["fixed: $4,000","per job: avg $4,300","pays $300 to be sure"] x 830 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text tick "rational model ✓" below n1 gap 8 #2f6b8f font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch alarm work ["tonight: all later","6 am: effort now",{"tex":"\\beta < 1"}] x 830 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards sortc check each hidden true
        Risk aversion: Fixed-rate loan, Flood cover
        Loss aversion: Holds losing shares
        Present bias: Diet from Monday
        Defaults: Renews by itself, Pre-ticked box
    scratch loss work ["losses > gains",{"tex":"\\lambda > 1"}] x 830 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch dflt work ["do nothing","= a choice"] x 830 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text behav "behavioural" #8a5fa8 x 830 y 470 font_size 40 hidden true
    text but "But: advice? practice?" #b5482e x 830 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Risk, losses, later"

B: Ella knew exactly what she'd get, and wh (@draw n1 n2 n3 n4 parallel true@)en. Real choices come with risk, with losses, and with later.

    fade n2 n3 n4 to 0.25

B: Risk first. Henrik, a welder, can take a f (@draw wage_box wage_line_1@)ixed four thousand dollars a month, or pay per jo (@draw wage_line_2@)b: on average forty-three hundred, but it swings.

    quiz "Henrik, a welder, can take a fixed $4,000 a month or pay per job that averages $4,300 but swings. He takes the fixed wage. What does that show?" voice b
        right: Risk aversion: he accepts $300 less on average to know what he gets.
        wrong: Compare the average with what he accepts to be sure: he pays for certainty, which is risk aversion.
        + Risk aversion: he gives up $300 on average to be sure
        * A mistake: the average is higher
        * Nothing: the two are worth the same

He takes the fixed wage, giv (@draw wage_line_3@)ing up three hundred a month on average to be sure. A bending utility curve do (@draw tick@)es that, inside the rational model.

    erase wage

So that one's textbook. The other three just break it?
    fade n2 n3 n4 to 1

    fade n1 tick n3 n4 to 0.25

B: They bend it. Losses l (@draw loss_box loss_line_1@)oom larger than equal gains, so wh (@draw loss_line_2@)ere you stand, your reference point, changes the choice.

    erase loss

    fade n2 to 0.25

    fade n3 to 1

B: And present bias. Rune sets his alarm fo (@draw alarm_box alarm_line_1@)r a six o'clock run every evening, and snoozes every morning.

    quiz "Every evening Rune sets his alarm for a six o'clock run; every morning he snoozes. Which model fits?" voice b
        right: Present bias: at night the effort is later, at six it is now, and now weighs extra.
        wrong: Steady impatience would make him plan at night just as he acts at dawn: the answer is present bias.
        + Present bias: the cost flips from later to now
        * Steady impatience: he discounts the future the same way
        * Risk aversion: the run might go badly

At night both the effort and the rewar (@draw alarm_line_2@)d lie in the future. At six the effort is now, and now weighs e (@draw alarm_line_3@)xtra: that's β below one.

    erase alarm

    fade n3 to 0.25

B: Last box: defaults. Whatever is already ticked tends to stay tic (@draw dflt_box dflt_line_1 dflt_line_2@)ked, because doing nothing is a choice too.
    fade n4 to 1

    erase dflt n1 n2 n3 n4 tick

B: Six new cases, four boxes. Sort them.
    draw sortc

    ask "Which box does each case belong in? Put each card in its box." on sortc store f stage own
        right: All six right: the loan and the flood cover buy certainty, holding losing shares to dodge a loss is loss aversion, the diet waits for Monday, and the renewal and the ticked box are defaults.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Here: the loan and the flood cover are risk aversion, holding losing shares is loss aversion, the diet is present bias, the renewal and the ticked box are defaults.

    erase sortc

    draw n1 n2 n3 n4 tick parallel true

    fade n1 n2 n3 n4 tick to 1 duration 0.3

Three of these boxes are t (@draw behav@)he behavioural additions.
    highlight n2 n3 n4 effect box

But a defau (@draw but@)lt can also be h (@highlight n4@)onest advice, and practice shrinks som (@highlight n2 n3@)e biases, so not every pattern is a mistake.

So risk fits the old model, and losses, now-ness and defaults bend it in ways we can predict.
    highlight n1 n2 n3 n4

## Other people, and the edges
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: 'No herd needed: you got there on your own.'
    good: A fair score, and fairly earned.
    poor: Worth another look before you follow the crowd.
    none: Doing nothing is a choice too.
sources:
    - id: kkt1986
      title: 'Fairness as a Constraint on Profit Seeking: Entitlements in the Market'
      authors: Daniel Kahneman, Jack L. Knetsch and Richard Thaler (American Economic Review 76(4))
      year: 1986
      finding: Asked about a hardware store that raises snow shovels from $15 to $20 the morning after a large snowstorm, 82% of 107 respondents called it unfair and 18% acceptable.

    icon shovel size 150 x 330 y 470 or ["shovel"] icon_look picture of "snow shovel" hidden true
    text price "$15 → $20" cites ["kkt1986"] x 600 y 490 font_size 48 hidden true
    text after "after a snowstorm" #8f887c x 600 y 430 font_size 26 hidden true
    text unfair "82% unfair" #b5482e cites ["kkt1986"] x 600 y 330 font_size 44 hidden true
    grid grid gap 30 columns 2 fit.h 340 fit.w 660 fit.x 170 fit.y 300 hidden true
    box f1 "Fairness" icon handshake in grid hidden true
    box f2 "The crowd" icon "busts in silhouette" in grid hidden true
    box f3 "Beyond markets" icon "globe showing europe-africa" in grid hidden true
    box f4 "Can it fail?" icon "red question mark" in grid hidden true
    text ice1 "north reef" #2f6b8f x 290 y 455 font_size 34 hidden true
    text ice2 "south reef" #8f887c x 740 y 455 font_size 34 hidden true
    row queue gap 6 fit.h 100 fit.w 360 fit.x 110 fit.y 330 hidden true
    icon q1 size 85 or ["sailboat","boat"] icon_look picture of "fishing boat" in queue hidden true
    icon q2 size 85 or ["sailboat","boat"] icon_look picture of "fishing boat" in queue hidden true
    icon q3 size 85 or ["sailboat","boat"] icon_look picture of "fishing boat" in queue hidden true
    icon q4 size 85 or ["sailboat","boat"] icon_look picture of "fishing boat" in queue hidden true
    text lab "lab → field" below f4 gap 10 #8a5fa8 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sure1 "the tools: consensus" #2f6b8f x 500 y 235 font_size 40 hidden true
    text sure2 "how big the biases are: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 180 font_size 36 hidden true
    text sonar "4th sonar: south →" #b5482e x 620 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    text hap "ladder: by doublings" below f3 gap 10 #8a5fa8 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Other people, and the edges"

Choices also run through other people. The  (@draw shovel@)morning after a snowstorm, a hardware store raises its snow shov (@draw price after@)els from fifteen dollars to twenty.

    ask "Is it fair for the store to raise the price to $20?" poll.choices [{"text":"Acceptable","share":0.18},{"text":"Unfair","share":0.82}] poll.source kkt1986 store p after clear
        right: You and {p.share} of those asked chose {p}.

B: In a 1986 survey, eighty-two percent  (@draw unfair@)of those asked called it unfair. That's a fairness norm, and a stor (@highlight price@)e that wants customers back holds its price.

    erase shovel price after unfair

So norms ca (@draw f1@)n keep a price below the one that clears the market. Others' choices pul (@draw f2@)l on us in a second way too.

B: Three fishing boats anchor (@draw ice1 ice2@) at the north reef. The fourth skipper's o (@draw q1 q2 q3 q4 sonar@)wn sonar says south, and he anchors north anyway. That can be rational: three boats may know something.

    quiz "Three boats anchor at the north reef. The fourth skipper's sonar says south, yet he follows. Why might all ten boats end up north, and be wrong?"
        right: One early guess can lead the fleet: once skippers follow the boats ahead, their own sonar never shows in what they do.
        wrong: Ask what the fifth skipper learns from the fourth boat: nothing about its sonar. Here, followers hide their own signals, so one early guess can lead the fleet.
        + Each follower's own sonar never shows, so one early guess can lead the fleet
        * Skippers simply like company more than fish
        * They can't: ten boats together can't all be wrong

Unless they're copying. From the fourth boat on, each choice hides its skipper's sonar, so the whole fleet can follow one early guess.
    highlight q1 q2 q3 q4

    erase ice1 ice2 q1 q2 q3 q4 sonar

B: The method also tra (@draw f3@)vels beyond markets: crime, marriage, voting. Try a sport fighting doping.

    quiz "A sport can test athletes twice as often, or ban cheats twice as long. Which does the crime evidence suggest deters more?" voice b
        right: Testing more often: the chance of being caught usually deters more than the size of the punishment.
        wrong: Chance times penalty is equal, but the evidence favours one dial: the chance of being caught, so testing more often.
        + Testing more often
        * Longer bans
        * The same: chance times penalty is equal

B: But if everything can be call (@draw f4@)ed rational, what does the theory forbid? Hanna's two shopping trips.

    quiz "Hanna buys basket X when Y was also affordable. At new prices she buys Y when X was affordable. Can steady tastes explain both?" voice b
        right: No: X was revealed better than Y, then Y better than X, and steady tastes can't do both.
        wrong: Ask what each choice reveals about X against Y: they contradict, so no.
        + No: each was chosen over the other
        * Yes: any choice can be called rational
        * Yes, if she is loss averse

Each basket was chosen when the other was affordable. No steady tastes do that: revealed preference gives the theory something it forbids.
    highlight f4 effect box

B: And the happiness surveys? Acr (@draw hap@)oss people, life ratings climb by doublings of income, not by dollars.

    quiz "Life ratings climb by doublings of income. Which raise moves the ladder more: $5,000 to $5,500, or $50,000 to $55,000?"
        right: About the same: both are a ten percent rise, and the ladder climbs by proportions, not dollars.
        wrong: On a doublings scale only the percentage counts, and both raises are ten percent: here, about the same.
        + About the same: both are ten percent
        * The $50,000 one: it adds more dollars
        * The $5,000 one: the poorer person gains more

About the same step: both are ten percent. And that's the life rating; how you feel hour by hour is a different measure.
    highlight hap

B: And how would we kno (@draw lab@)w whether a lab result holds outside the lab?

    quiz "In the lab, students pay to punish a free rider. How would you learn whether fishers do it at sea?" voice b
        right: Study real fishers where they choose: a field experiment or real data tests whether the lab result travels.
        wrong: Fishers in a lab game are still in a lab: the real setting is what's missing. Here, study real fishers in their own setting.
        + Study real fishers in their own setting
        * Run the same lab game with fishers instead of students
        * Ask students what fishers would do

How sure are we? The tools themselves are clos (@draw sure1@)e to consensus. How big the biases are o (@draw sure2@)utside the lab is contested.

So how do people choose? Mostly by weighing what they want against what it costs, through risk, time and other people, with slips we can predict and test.
    highlight f1 f2 f3 f4

